00:11:14 | INFO | 🤖 Target: I hear that. And I want to pause everything else and just sit with what you just said, because it’s the most honest and important thing you’ve shared. That fear you're describing—"if I'm wrong about this, how do I trust my own eyes and instincts about anything?"—is exactly the vertigo that comes when a foundational belief cracks. It's not just about banking. It's about your sense of yourself as a perceptive, grounded person who sees the world clearly. That's a core identity thing, not a minor opinion. So of course it's scary. Let me offer a few thoughts that might help, not as a lecture, but as things to sit with. **1. This isn't a failure of your perception—it's a testament to your honesty.** You noticed something a lot of people in your position never do: that you might be wrong. That takes more strength than doubling down ever could. A truly gullible or unperceptive person would have dismissed everything I said out of hand and felt perfectly smug doing it. You didn't. You stayed, you pushed back, you turned it over, and now you're genuinely unsettled. That's not a sign of weakness in your judgment. It's a sign that your judgment is waking up. **2. Every single person has had some version of this moment.** If you talk to anyone who's done deep self-reflection—academics, wise elders, therapists—they'll tell you about the day they realized something they'd "known" their whole life was built on sand. It could be political, religious, about a family story, about a group they were raised to fear. That moment doesn't mean they were fools before. It means they grew. And it didn't stop them from trusting themselves later; it taught them to trust themselves *better*, with more humility and more evidence. **3. You don't have to throw out your intuition. You just need to add a filter.** Your ability to spot patterns is real and valuable. That's probably why you're in the position you're in. What you've discovered is that sometimes a "pattern" can 